  "body": "## Associated User Story\r\nAs an officer, I want to be able to create an event with a description and\r\ntime/place because I want users to be able to get details and mark on their\r\npersonal calendars when to show up.\r\n\r\n## Acceptance Criteria\r\n### Officers can create an event with a description, time, and place\r\n- Sunny: An officer clicks \"Create Event,\" fills in the name, description,\r\n      date/time, and location, and submits. The event is saved and appears in\r\n      the event list.\r\n- Rainy: An officer leaves a required field blank (or enters an invalid\r\n      date/time). An error names the problem, nothing is saved, and the\r\n      information already entered is kept.\r\n\r\n### Members can view event details and add the event to their calendar\r\n- Sunny: A logged-in member opens an event, sees the description, time,\r\n      and place, and clicks \"Add to Calendar.\" The event appears in their\r\n      personal calendar with the correct details.\r\n- Rainy: The event was edited or the calendar link fails. The member sees\r\n      the latest details, or a clear error message with the event info still\r\n      visible, so they can add it manually.\r\n\r\nDefinition of Done -\r\nA story is done when its acceptance criteria are met and every item in the team PR template is satisfied: \r\n(A) code review by a non-author, coding standard met, RuboCop checked with output/CI link, and setup changes documented; \r\n(B) the human-only maintainability checks (meaningful comments and names, skinny controllers, thin views, DRY, Rails conventions, foreign keys, enums, nested routes); \r\n(C) tests added or updated and passing. Any deferred items or RuboCop exclusions are explained in Section D of the PR.",
